<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Brian Boru</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 941–<a href="/years/1014">1014</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Brian Bóruma, Brian Bóruma mac Cennétig</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Brian Boru was King of Munster from the Dál Cais dynasty and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/high-king-of-ireland">High King of Ireland</a> from 1002. He broke the power of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> towns and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eoganachta">Eóganachta</a>, then forced the other provincial kings to accept his overlordship. He was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-clontarf">Battle of Clontarf</a> in 1014, where his army defeated Leinster and its Norse allies.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/brian-boru.jpg" alt="Brian Boru" width="320" height="505" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brian_B%C3%B3ruma_mac_Cenn%C3%A9tig_(1723)_(crop).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Norse raiders built a fortified base at Dublin in 841, which grew into the chief Norse town and slave market of Ireland; <b>Brian Boru</b>’s victory at Clontarf in 1014 was later remembered as the end of Viking power there.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vikings">Vikings · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>The Irish high king <b>Brian Boru</b> defeated the forces of Leinster and their Norse allies from Dublin and beyond.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-23">23 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Probably near Lyons Hill in County Kildare, the forces of Munster and Meath under <b>Brian Boru</b> and the High King Máel Sechnaill II crushed the armies of Leinster and Dublin led by King Máel Mórda, ending the brief Leinster revolt.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-30">30 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>A northern branch, the Déis Tuaiscirt, later grew into the Dál Cais, the dynasty of <b>Brian Bóruma</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/deisi-muman">Deisi Muman</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Brian Boru</b> of Munster broke the Ui Neill monopoly around 1002, and later kings ruled 'with opposition'.</blockquote><a href="/state/high-kingship-of-ireland">High Kingship of Ireland</a></li><li><blockquote>Mathgamain and <b>Brian Boru</b> of the Dal Cais defeated the Norse at Sulcoit in 967 and sacked the town.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-limerick">Kingdom of Limerick</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Killaloe, in what is now <a href="/countries/ireland">Ireland</a>. Died in Clontarf, in what is now <a href="/countries/ireland">Ireland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.8,-8.45&amp;year=988" aria-label="Killaloe on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4361 344 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Brian Boru"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4811 614h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4811 614h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.8,-8.45&amp;year=988">What was here in 988 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
